{
  "requirement": "`ToolUsage` holds the cumulative usage for one tool.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct ToolUsage {\n    pub calls: u64,\n    pub result_bytes: u64,\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for ToolUsage;\nimpl Copy for ToolUsage;\nimpl Debug for ToolUsage;\nimpl Default for ToolUsage;\nimpl Serialize for ToolUsage;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`ToolUsage` is a declaration-only requirement. It specifies a `#[non_exhaustive]` struct with public `u64` fields `calls` and `result_bytes`, plus the trait impls Clone, Copy, Debug, Default and Serialize. The behavior of the counters is refined by `ToolUsage.calls`, `ToolUsage.result_bytes` and `ToolUsage.replay`, and those need no test here.\n\n`tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` covers the declaration:\n- `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md` lists `struct ToolUsage` and links it to this requirement's declaration. `specified_exports` parses that entry. `documented_exports` panics with \"Missing inventory declaration\" if rustdoc exports a struct that isn't listed, and the test also fails if the listed export is absent from rustdoc.\n- `check_declaration` extracts the requirement's Rust listing and its trait-impl listing. It asserts that the set of normalized `impl ... for ToolUsage` lines equals the impls rustdoc shows (`trait_impls`). An added or missing Clone, Copy, Debug, Default or Serialize impl fails the test.\n- `check_declaration` compares the requirement's declaration with rustdoc's declaration through `signature_parts`. That comparison covers the struct form, the field names and types (`calls: u64`, `result_bytes: u64`), their `pub` visibility, and the `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute. It also asserts that a struct with public fields shows `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- The test also checks that the SDK compiles under the Habeas consumer and that the feature set is empty. Neither check bears on this requirement.\n\nI found no behavioral obligation in this requirement's own text that the tests miss. The text doesn't specify Serialize's output format or Default's values. The implementation (`graph.rs#struct[ToolUsage]`) derives all five traits and carries `#[non_exhaustive]`, which matches what the test enforces. I read that implementation only through a search snippet.",
  "references": "0d76678f3ab137bf77b6a76d0cd17c460f60426827a412971b8cd589c65e25da",
